The last row is the one discrepancy. The paper's Figure 2 reports +25% decode FLOPs from 4K to 1M
context; our op accounting gives +{{ (w.validation[-1].ours*100)|n(0) }}%. The growth comes almost
entirely from indexer scoring: the layer-20 Full indexer scans the whole context, and the three
encoder Full indexers scan half of it. We take the extra growth to be a convention difference
(for example, whether the index-score reduction is counted). It does not matter at the measured
~2.6K context, where the indexer is under 1% of a step.

## 3. Decode: FLOPs and bytes per output token

One step drafts 5 tokens, verifies 6 rows through all 40 layers and emits {{ w.decode_step.accept_len }} tokens on
average. With independent routing, the 6 verify rows touch **{{ w.decode_step.distinct_experts_verify|n(1) }}
distinct experts per layer**. The drafter touches {{ w.decode_step.distinct_experts_draft|n(1) }} per block. One step reads
**{{ (w.decode_step.unique_bytes/1e9)|n(1) }} GB** of unique bytes and performs {{ (w.decode_step.flops/1e9)|n(0) }} GFLOP.
Normalized per emitted token (system-wide, batch 1, context 2.6K):

| op class | FLOPs / token | HBM bytes / token | FLOP/B | bound on B300 (FP8 ridge {{ w.ridge.fp8|n(0) }} FLOP/B) |
|---|---|---|---|---|
{% for r in w.decode_table %}
{% if r.category != 'misc' %}
| {{ r.label }} | {{ r.flops_per_token|si('FLOP') }} | {{ r.bytes_per_token|si('B') }}{% if r.coll_bytes_per_token > 0 %} (+{{ r.coll_bytes_per_token|si('B') }} on links){% endif %} | {% if r.bytes_per_token > 0 %}{{ r.intensity|n(1) }}{% else %}-{% endif %} | {% if r.bytes_per_token == 0 %}latency{% elif r.intensity < 100 %}memory ({{ (w.ridge.fp8 / r.intensity)|n(0) }}x below ridge){% else %}near ridge{% endif %} |
{% endif %}
{% endfor %}

Notes:
* **Routed experts dominate.** They are {{ pct(w.decode_table|selectattr('category','equalto','moe_routed')|map(attribute='bytes_per_token')|first / (w.decode_step.unique_bytes / w.decode_step.accept_len)) }} of all bytes and run at about 4 FLOP/B: each touched expert serves about one row.
* **Attention core** (one shared 512-wide latent, 64 heads) has high arithmetic intensity, because the KV is tiny: {{ si(w.decode_table|selectattr('category','equalto','attn_core')|map(attribute='bytes_per_token')|first, 'B') }} per token.
* **All-to-all vs all-reduce.** Expert parallelism would move {{ w.ep_alltoall_bytes_per_layer|si('B') }} per layer (dispatch + combine of 6 rows x 6 experts x 5,120 x BF16). The engine's two TP all-reduces move {{ w.tp_allreduce_bytes_per_layer|si('B') }} per layer. Both are tiny, so at batch 1 collectives cost latency, not bandwidth.
* **Engram is a capacity problem, not a bandwidth one.** The two tables hold {{ (w.engram.table_bytes/1e9)|n(0) }} GB (sharded into GPU HBM by the engine, {{ (w.engram.table_bytes/4/1e9)|n(0) }} GB per GB300). A step looks up only {{ w.engram.lookup_bytes_per_step|si('B') }}: about {{ (w.engram.lookup_bytes_per_step / (K.meas4.step_ms/1e3) / 1e6)|n(0) }} MB/s. The addresses are known from the token ids before the step starts (tech report §2.4.2).

