## 4. Prefill: per prompt token

With CED, prefill runs only the 20 encoder layers over each prompt token. It then projects the
decoder's global KV from the encoder output and replays only the last 128 tokens through the decoder
(tech report §2.2, §3.2.2). Per prompt token at position 1M, in 8K-token chunks:

| op class | FLOPs / token | HBM bytes / token (amortized over the chunk) |
|---|---|---|
{% for r in w.prefill_table_1m %}
{% if r.flops_per_token > 0 %}
| {{ r.label }} | {{ r.flops_per_token|si('FLOP') }} | {{ r.bytes_per_token|si('B') }} |
{% endif %}
{% endfor %}

Prefill is compute-bound everywhere except the routed experts. An 8K chunk touches all 384 experts
per layer, which puts them near the ridge. The quadratic indexer term is the largest compute item
at 1M context. Reported 1M-token prefill is {{ K.meas4.prefill_1m_s }} s. Our roofline model says
{{ P.prefill.fit_k_tp4|n(1) }}x less (section 3 of [04](04_results.md#prefill-and-ttft)), so prefill
is *not* a calibrated part of this study.

## 5. Roofline on the GPU baseline

![roofline](../results/fig_roofline.png)

Batch-1 decode ops sit 20-150x left of the B300 FP8 ridge ({{ w.ridge.fp8|n(0) }} FLOP/B). Even at
batch 64 the routed experts stay memory-bound, because each expert still sees only about 6 rows.
Prefill's dense GEMMs sit far to the right. Decode is a memory-bandwidth problem in the roofline
sense. Section 6 shows that, at batch 1, it is *also* a latency problem.

![KV share](../results/fig_decode_bytes_vs_context.png)

CSA2 + FP4 KV keep KV traffic negligible: under 1% of step bytes at batch 1, even at 1M tokens. The
indexer scan only matters for long context *and* large batch.

## 6. The bottleneck is not where the roofline says

At 3.30 ms per step the 4 x GB300 average {{ pct(w.decode_per_gpu_tp4.total/1e9/K.meas4.step_ms/8.0) }} of
peak HBM bandwidth. Calibrating the execution model to the two measured step times
([06_simulator.md](06_simulator.md#calibration)) splits each step into a part that scales
with bytes per GPU and a part that does not:

| | 4 x GB300 (3.30 ms) | 8 x B300 (2.71 ms) |
|---|---|---|
| weight/KV streaming | {{ K.mem4_ms|n(2) }} ms | {{ K.mem8_ms|n(2) }} ms |
| per-stage fixed latency + collective latency | **{{ K.fixed4_ms|n(2) }} ms ({{ K.fixed4_share|pct }})** | **{{ K.fixed8_ms|n(2) }} ms ({{ K.fixed8_share|pct }})** |

The fixed part is about {{ ((K.fixed4_ms*1000)/43)|n(0) }} µs per transformer block, across about
{{ cal.stages_per_block|n(0) }} dependent kernel stages including the fused all-reduces. With PDL these
are not launch gaps. They are the latency of dependent stages: each kernel's output must be complete
across the GPU before the next can consume it, plus ramp, tail and collective latency. The fitted
value, {{ cal.kernel_overhead_us|n(1) }} µs per stage, is consistent with published measurements
([sources](sources.md): >1 µs per device-wide barrier, 2.4-5 µs per small 4-GPU all-reduce).

**How sure are we?** The two data points pin the *sum* of fixed costs. They do not pin the split
between kernel stages and collectives, nor the bandwidth efficiency, tightly. Refitting under every
alternative we could think of moves the fixed share of the TP4 step between
**{{ K.fixed_lo|pct }} and {{ K.fixed_hi|pct }}** ([04](04_results.md#calibration)). The alternatives
are: per-peer all-reduce growth, routing locality, and the README's own run-to-run extremes. The
range is consistent with TP8 being only {{ K.tp8_gain|pct }} faster per step than TP4. That number is an
input to the fit, though, not independent evidence. A single profile
(`tools/model_vs_nsys.py` next to the engine's `tools/nsys_breakdown.py`) would settle it.
